== [3] The offset anchor: the old day vs the seeded day, at the runner's offset
-- scripts/t39_offset_anchor_seed.py writes #t39-anchor; the two columns are the same query asked
-- with the offset in seconds (-18000) and with it read as minutes (-300).

runner offset -18000s; #t34-showcase newest post sydog9nbci84tncjmpgsgagf6a = 2026-10-27 in that offset
  old anchor (the newest post's day)           in:t34-showcase on:2026-10-27        seconds=26 minutes= 2  SEPARATES — the test measures
  new anchor (seeded fixture)                  in:t39-anchor on:2026-10-26          seconds= 3 minutes= 0  SEPARATES — the test measures

-- The old anchor is separating again *now* only because other cards have posted 26 messages into
-- #t34-showcase and #t36-threads today; T55-AV-R measured the same day answering 2 and 2 at -25200
-- (build/logs/t55/t55-tz-separation.log) a few hours earlier, and the day before that 1 vs 3. That
-- is the defect: the anchor moved with the fixture and the wall clock.

== [4] The seeded day at other plausible runner offsets (fixture written for -18000s)

  offset  -18000s: seconds= 3 minutes= 0 separates
  offset  -14400s: seconds= 3 minutes= 0 separates
  offset  -21600s: seconds= 3 minutes= 0 separates
  offset  -25200s: seconds= 3 minutes= 0 separates
  offset  -28800s: seconds= 3 minutes= 0 separates
  offset       0s: seconds= 0 minutes= 0 SAME — the test would skip
  offset    3600s: seconds= 0 minutes= 0 SAME — the test would skip

-- i.e. it survives a DST change in any US zone; a UTC or eastern runner must re-run the seed with
-- its own --tz-offset-seconds, and the test says so in its skip message.

